    "summary": "This PR replaces the single flat color-history drawer with a palette model: palettes[0] is the persistent auto-history, named palettes are appended, and Eyedroppers gains save/rename/delete/add-chip/delete-chip operations plus a tabbed PaletteTabBar in the drawer. The data-layer plumbing (autoHistory computed accessor, activePairs, activeHistoryID-based swap/delete) was rewritten, so the main review focus is index bookkeeping for Defaults[.activePaletteIndex] and the consistency of activeHistoryID with the currently active palette, since several code paths now early-return or act on the wrong palette when those two values drift.",
    "risk_level": "high",
    "rule_findings": [],
    "suggestions": [
      {
        "file_path": "Pika/Services/Eyedroppers.swift",
        "line": 289,
        "severity": "high",
        "confidence": 0.7,
        "title": "deletePalette only clamps the active index, so deleting a palette before the active one jumps to auto-history",
        "reason": "The added body of deletePalette ends with `if Defaults[.activePaletteIndex] >= palettes.count { Defaults[.activePaletteIndex] = 0 }` after `palettes.remove(at: index)`. It never decrements activePaletteIndex when the deleted palette sits before the active one, and it never calls restoreAutoHistorySelection() even though the PR description claims it does. Since the tab bar draws selection from `index == activePaletteIndex`, removing an earlier palette either clamps to 0 (auto-history) or leaves the index pointing at a different palette than the user selected.",
        "recommendation": "Decrement the active index when index < activePaletteIndex, and re-sync the selection after removal, e.g.:\n\n```swift\nfunc deletePalette(at index: Int) {\n    var palettes = Defaults[.palettes]\n    guard index > 0, index < palettes.count else { return }\n    let wasActive = Defaults[.activePaletteIndex]\n    palettes.remove(at: index)\n    Defaults[.palettes] = palettes\n    if wasActive == index || wasActive >= palettes.count {\n        Defaults[.activePaletteIndex] = 0\n        restoreAutoHistorySelection()\n    } else if wasActive > index {\n        Defaults[.activePaletteIndex] = wasActive - 1\n    }\n}\n```",
        "source": "ai",
        "evidence": [
          "        if Defaults[.activePaletteIndex] >= palettes.count {"
        ],
        "failure_scenario": "Palettes are [auto, A, B] with B active (activePaletteIndex == 2). The user right-clicks A and chooses Delete: palettes become [auto, B] and `2 >= 2` sets activePaletteIndex to 0, so the drawer silently switches to the auto-history tab (with activeHistoryID still referencing B's chip), instead of keeping B selected."
      }
    ],
